_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_

**Apply `>>> 0` per iteration to ensure correct 32-bit unsigned behavior.**

The current implementation applies `>>> 0` only at the end. In JavaScript, while `acc << 5` truncates to 32-bit, the subsequent `+ acc` re-adds the potentially large accumulator value. For file paths (which are much longer than ~11 characters), the accumulator can exceed JavaScript's safe integer limit (2^53), causing precision loss and potentially inconsistent hash results.

The linked issue `#34270` explicitly suggests applying `>>> 0` inside the loop:
> `hash = ((hash << 5) + hash + charCode) >>> 0`



<details>
<summary>Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
 /** djb2 hash — http://www.cse.yorku.ca/~oz/hash.html */
 function hash(value: string) {
   let acc = 5381;
   for (let i = 0; i < value.length; i++) {
-    acc = (acc << 5) + acc + value.charCodeAt(i);
+    acc = ((acc << 5) + acc + value.charCodeAt(i)) >>> 0;
   }
-  return acc >>> 0;
+  return acc;
 }
```

</details>

